{
  "specVersion": 1,
  "title": "Nucleus live data-model conformance",
  "why": [
    "Every serious bug found on 2026-08-11 lived at the client boundary. The engine's own testing is excellent and caught none of them: 4,200+ unit tests, a differential fuzzer at zero divergences against SQLite, 49 probes. Eighteen call shapes had NEVER worked over pgwire from Python while every one of them had a green test, because the tests were mocked. A mock proves the client builds the SQL the test expects. It cannot know the server describes that statement with zero result columns, or types a parameter as TEXT that the client binds as an integer.",
    "One of seven SDKs has a live suite. Six ports written by hand would drift the moment one of them fixed an encoding question the others did not. So the cases live here as data, and every SDK ships a thin executor that maps op names to its own client. Adding a case is a change to this file, and all seven pick it up.",
    "The cross-SDK behavioural diff falls out of the same structure: run the same spec through seven executors, compare the recorded results, and any disagreement is drift by construction."
  ],
  "conventions": {
    "args": "Positional. A string of the form '@name' is a per-case unique fixture name (stable within a case, unique across runs, so cases never depend on a clean database or on ordering). A string of the form '$name' resolves to a value bound by an earlier step. Anything else is a literal.",
    "bind": "Names the step's result so later steps can reference it as '$name'.",
    "expect": "Omitted means the step's result is not asserted, only that the call survived the wire. See the expectation vocabulary below.",
    "xfail": "The case is expected to FAIL. Strict: if it passes, the run fails, so nobody fixes the underlying bug without noticing that the note here is now stale. An optional `sdks` list scopes the expectation to named SDKs — some engine defects are only observable through a particular driver strategy, and without scoping every unaffected SDK reports xpass forever and the signal is lost."
  },
  "expectations": {
    "key": "Read this field out of a map result before comparing.",
    "index": "Read this position out of a list result before comparing.",
    "jsonDecode": "Parse a string result as JSON before comparing. Pass the value through unchanged when it is NOT a string: several drivers decode jsonb themselves, and a literal implementation reports '[object Object] is not valid JSON' and hides the actual value. The point is to compare the VALUE, whichever side of the wire decoded it.",
    "equals": "Deep equality against the given JSON value.",
    "notNull": "Result is present.",
    "isNull": "Result is absent (null/None/nil/undefined, as the language spells it).",
    "nonEmpty": "Result is a collection with at least one element.",
    "length": "Result is a collection of exactly this length.",
    "type": "One of list, map, string, int, float, bool, bytes."
  },
  "cases": [
    {
      "id": "features.detection",
      "model": "core",
      "why": "If this reports PostgreSQL then every specialty assertion below tested the wrong server and is silently meaningless.",
      "steps": [
