    {
      "id": "kv.values_containing_a_comma_survive_the_round_trip",
      "model": "kv",
      "why": "conformance/README.md records a comma-split bug as CONFIRMED across Go, Python, Rust and TypeScript: KV reads come back comma-separated and every client splits on ',', so a member containing a comma is silently torn in two. Measured against current main on 2026-08-13 with the DISCRIMINATING shape — two members, one containing a comma, so a naive split yields three — Python returns both members intact for lrange, smembers and hgetall. The finding is stale for Python. The other six executors are how we learn whether it is stale for them.",
      "steps": [
        {
          "op": "kv.rpush",
          "args": [
            "@list",
            "a,b"
          ]
        },
        {
          "op": "kv.rpush",
          "args": [
            "@list",
            "c"
          ]
        },
        {
          "op": "kv.lrange",
          "args": [
            "@list",
            0,
            10
          ],
          "expect": {
            "equals": [
              "a,b",
              "c"
            ]
          }
        },
        {
          "op": "kv.sadd",
          "args": [
            "@set",
            "x,y"
          ]
        },
        {
          "op": "kv.sadd",
          "args": [
            "@set",
            "z"
          ]
        },
        {
          "op": "kv.smembers",
          "args": [
            "@set"
          ],
          "expect": {
            "length": 2
          }
        },
        {
          "op": "kv.hset",
          "args": [
            "@hash",
            "f1",
            "v,1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "op": "kv.hset",
          "args": [
            "@hash",
            "f2",
            "v2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "op": "kv.hgetall",
          "args": [
            "@hash"
          ],
          "expect": {
            "key": "f1",
            "equals": "v,1"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "kv.zrange_members_are_not_delimiter_joined",
      "model": "kv",
      "why": "MISATTRIBUTED until 2026-08-15, and the correction is the point. The note blamed KV_ZRANGE for joining member and score with ':'. The engine does no such thing — it answers with clean JSON pairs, [[\"a:b\",1.0]] — and four CLIENTS parsed those pairs and then re-joined them with ':', reintroducing the ambiguity themselves. Rust returned members all along, which is how the misattribution surfaced: a fifth SDK passing a case four others xfail is evidence about the note, not about the SDK. Fixed in python, go, typescript and elixir; the xfail is gone because the bug is.",
      "steps": [
        {
          "op": "kv.zadd",
          "args": [
            "@zset",
            1.0,
            "a:b"
          ]
        },
        {
          "op": "kv.zrange",
          "args": [
            "@zset",
            0,
            10
          ],
          "expect": {
            "index": 0,
            "equals": "a:b"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
