    {
      "id": "vector.insert_and_search",
      "model": "vector",
      "why": "FIXED 2026-08-13, engine-side. Was not a vector bug at all — silent data loss. A VECTOR column declared with NO dimension produces tuples the storage layer cannot deserialize; the engine logs `failed to deserialize tuple on page 2 (slot 0); row omitted from scan` and every scan that touches the heap drops every row, while PK/index-only reads still report them. Measured in psql: on such a table `SELECT id` returns 2 rows and `COUNT(*)` returns 2, while `SELECT *`, `SELECT embedding`, `SELECT metadata` and `SELECT id, metadata` each return 0. With `VECTOR(3)` everything returns both rows through every client. Two reads disagreeing about how many rows a table holds, with no error raised, is the worst failure mode this engine has. VECTOR_DISTANCE was a red herring — `SELECT *` loses the data just as thoroughly. Reached from Python because its create_collection accepted a dimension and emitted a bare VECTOR column; fixed on the SDK side, but the engine defect stands.",
      "steps": [
        {
          "op": "vector.createCollection",
          "args": [
            "@coll",
            3
          ]
        },
        {
          "op": "vector.insert",
          "args": [
            "@coll",
            "v1",
            [
              1.0,
              0.0,
              0.0
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "op": "vector.insert",
          "args": [
            "@coll",
            "v2",
            [
              0.0,
              1.0,
              0.0
            ]
          ]
        },
        {
          "op": "vector.count",
          "args": [
            "@coll"
          ],
          "expect": {
            "equals": 2
          }
        },
        {
          "op": "vector.search",
          "args": [
            "@coll",
            [
              1.0,
              0.0,
              0.0
            ],
            1
          ],
          "expect": {
            "length": 1
          }
        }
      ]
    },
